Anonymous Inner Class

Interview Questions

1. What is an Anonymous Inner Class?
-->An Anonymous Inner Class is an inner class without a name. It is declared and instantiated in a single statement. 
    It is mainly used when an object is needed only once.

      Example:
      
      interface Animal {
          void sound();
      }
      
      public class Test {
          public static void main(String[] args) {
              Animal a = new Animal() {
                  public void sound() {
                      System.out.println("Dog barks");
                  }
              };
      
              a.sound();
          }
      }





2. Why does it not need a name?
-->It doesn't need a name because:

      It is used only once.
      We create the object immediately after defining the class.
      There is no need to reuse the class later.




3. Can we create a constructor inside an anonymous class?
-->No.
      Since an anonymous class has no name, we cannot declare a constructor.
      
      Instead, we use an instance initializer block if initialization is required.
      
      Example:
      
      Runnable r = new Runnable() {
      
          {
              System.out.println("Object Created");
          }
      
          public void run() {
              System.out.println("Running...");
          }
      };





4. Can an anonymous inner class implement multiple interfaces?
-->No.
      An anonymous inner class can:
      
      Extend one class, or
      Implement one interface.
      
      It cannot implement multiple interfaces at the same time.





5. Is an anonymous inner class compiled? If yes, what is the file name?
-->Yes.
      The Java compiler creates a separate .class file.
      
      If the outer class is Test, the generated file will be:
      
      Test$1.class
      
      If there are multiple anonymous classes:
      
      Test$1.class
      Test$2.class
      Test$3.class



6. What is the disadvantage of an anonymous inner class?
-->Disadvantages:
      Cannot have a constructor.
      Cannot be reused.
      Harder to read for large code.
      Suitable only for one-time use.
      Cannot implement multiple interfaces.




7. What is the difference between a lambda expression and an anonymous inner class?
-->
| Anonymous Inner Class                         | Lambda Expression                     |
| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Introduced in Java 1.1                        | Introduced in Java 8                  |
| Can extend a class or implement one interface | Works only with functional interfaces |
| More code                                     | Less code                             |
| Can define fields and methods                 | Cannot define new methods or fields   |
| Has its own `this` reference                  | `this` refers to the enclosing class  |
| Slower and more verbose                       | Faster and concise                    |

Anonymous Inner Class:

Runnable r = new Runnable() {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Running");
    }
};

Lambda Expression:

Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("Running");
